 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers hold a self-hosted WebSocket connection and receive a revisioned view of the board as committed changes are published to it. The board runs its own connection infrastructure rather than paying a hosted realtime vendor: a market coordinator's board carries no per-connection revenue to justify a recurring provider fee, and the tenets already require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, which self-hosted sockets can satisfy directly. In exchange, the project owns connection capacity planning and reconnect handling rather than delegating them to a vendor.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store needs replayable revisions and reliable publication so a reconnecting browser can catch up to the latest state; delivery owns socket session lifecycle; browsers need reconnect-and-catch-up logic rather than a bare subscription call.
 
